{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>For when shall I entreat for you</b> Rashi wrote: if it had been said מָתַי אַעְתִּיר, it would mean \"when shall I pray\"; now that it says לְמָתַי, the meaning is \"I shall pray for you today that they be cut off at the time you set for me — say for which day you want them cut off.\" And according to the plain sense, at the time of his prayer the plagues would cease, for it is written, \"And Moses cried out to YHWH… and YHWH did\" — and it is not written \"and YHWH did on the morrow.\" And the expression <b>לְמָתַי</b> is no proof that he prayed immediately, for it is equivalent to מָתַי, and there are many lameds that come thus: \"tomorrow shall this sign be\" (Exodus 8:19), \"from the day that you came out of the land of Egypt\" (Deuteronomy 9:7), \"at the evening offering\" (Ezra 9:4), \"and the one wing to five cubits\" (II Chronicles 3:11), and many like these." "And the sense of <b>to cut off the frogs</b> is an allusion to their death, as in the expression \"that soul shall be cut off\" (Exodus 12:19), \"and I will cut off from Ahab\" (I Kings 21:21). And Moses said a second time, \"and the frogs shall depart,\" meaning that as soon as he prayed they would all depart, so that Pharaoh would not fear that these would die and others would come up from the Nile; rather, the plague would cease entirely, even though some of them would remain in the Nile. And all this was to inform him that the plague was from the God on account of Israel alone."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "Ramban writes הלמדי\"ן, literally 'the letters lamed-yod-nun', referring to instances of the prefixed lamed; rendered as 'lameds' for readability."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:30.582463884Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}